All work must be accessible. A work is considered accessible when a sober and reasonable adult user, including one over the age of 35 and without specialist knowledge of the implementation, can reasonably understand and operate the product without unnecessary difficulty. Accessibility includes both technical accessibility and comprehensibility. Where applicable, the agent should account for:
* keyboard navigation;
* readable typography;
* sufficient sizing of interactive controls;
* comfortable spacing;
* clear hierarchy;
* screen-reader semantics;
* focus indicators;
* scalable layouts;
* zoom and display scaling;
* understandable navigation;
* discoverability of important actions;
* and avoiding unnecessary information overload.


Interfaces should use appropriate abstraction. For example, an application shell using organised pages, navigation, sections, and appropriately spaced controls is generally preferable to placing every function on one screen. Accessibility must not be treated as a cosmetic afterthought.


6. Professional Quality
All work must be professional. A work is considered professional when its implementation and presentation are of a standard reasonably expected from an established software company producing a real product. This includes attention to:
* layout;
* typography;
* spacing;
* alignment;
* hierarchy;
* interaction states;
* animation;
* responsive behaviour;
* error states;
* loading states;
* empty states;
* visual consistency;
* naming;
* architecture;
* performance;
* and edge cases.
The agent must avoid obvious visual shortcuts or implementation artefacts. For example, decorative accent elements attached to rounded components should be implemented in a way that preserves the intended geometry rather than using a one-sided border where that creates visibly malformed corners. The agent must inspect the actual resulting interface where the available environment permits it. Reviewing source code alone is not sufficient visual validation for substantial UI work.
